From 5cdb047bef1a1ed34e084b22d09141f01917c096 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Will Nelson <will.nelson@discordapp.com>
Date: Mon, 16 Nov 2026 13:50:39 -0800
Subject: [PATCH 1/2] tree-sitter: publish the parser's error-recovery work
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset=UTF-8
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

Teach arborium's vendored copy of the tree-sitter C runtime — the one
this repo actually runs, compiled into the emscripten MAIN_MODULE host by
`build wasm host` and statically into the Node addon by lib/node/build.rs
— to publish how much work a parse is doing beyond the operations its
progress callback counts, and expose it on the Rust binding.

- include/tree_sitter/api.h, src/parser.c, src/lexer.{c,h}: add four
  cumulative counters to TSParseState, after the existing fields so
  readers of those stay layout-compatible:
  - reduction_scan_count: parse-table entries looked up by
    ts_parser__do_all_potential_reductions, which searches every
    reduction possible in a state (each lookup scans a row of the sparse
    table);
  - popped_subtree_count: subtrees ts_parser__reduce pops off the stack,
    summed over every stack path the reduction follows;
  - lexed_char_count: characters the lexer advanced over. Counted in
    ts_lexer__advance, which is the `advance` both the generated DFA
    lexer and external scanners call, on a saturating counter in Lexer;
    ts_parser__check_progress publishes it each time it consults the
    progress callback. One operation lexes one token however long it is,
    and error recovery re-lexes text, so this isn't bounded by either the
    operation count or the input length. (ts_lexer__get_column re-walks
    the line through ts_lexer__do_advance directly and isn't counted.)
  - summarized_child_count: children of the nodes the parser builds,
    each of which ts_subtree_summarize_children walks. Counted, on a
    saturating counter in TSParser, at each place parser.c calls
    ts_subtree_new_node / ts_subtree_new_error_node, from the size of the
    children array it passes. (Summaries redone while balancing the
    finished tree, in subtree.c, aren't counted; balancing already checks
    progress per operation.)
  All four are zeroed by ts_parser_reset, so they count from the start
  of the current parse.
- binding_rust/{bindings,lib}.rs: mirror the fields and expose them as
  ParseState::reduction_scan_count() / popped_subtree_count() /
  lexed_char_count() / summarized_child_count().

Why: the progress callback fires once per 100 parser operations, but an
operation's cost isn't fixed. In error recovery one operation can search
every reduction in a state and pop subtrees along many merged paths
through the stack. Profiling inputs that keep the parser recovering
(kotlin on repeated `-}`, Rust pasted as TypeScript, Markdown parsed as
JavaScript) put most of their time in exactly those two places, and
parse time per operation runs 5–10× that of ordinary source. Worse,
recovery can keep growing one ERROR node: skipped tokens are marked
extra, so each skip pops every extra token above the last ERROR and
rebuilds the ERROR from all of them. An unterminated 50 KB javascript
template literal summarizes ~313e6 children that way over ~1e5
operations. A meter that counts operations alone therefore under-charges
recovery-heavy input by up to ~8×, and the rebuilding case by far more;
weighting by these counters brings the spread to ~2×.
arborium-plugin-runtime's parse fuel (next patch) weights by them.
